ommit violence not just in Latin  America but but why you I feel that  these people are violent not because  they're Latin but because they're human  and the book has a lot of that in it but  it's not a glitzy book it doesn't have a  lot of famous people in it and I think  that had I gone with the other publisher  and tried to write a more commercial  book it would not have been as  interesting were there other countries  you could have put in this book you got  six in this one sure where there are  others and who would which countries  would they have been well the problem in  El Salvador is very similar to the  problem  Guatemala the control of the country the  stranglehold that the oligarchy has it's  probably worse in Guatemala than it is  in El Salvador I could have written  about Guatemala I could have written  about Mexico which has tremendous  repression and tremendous human rights  violations and doesn't tend to get  talked about in the United States why do  we spend so little time and maybe I'm  prejudicing your answer by sivan  suggestion is on the South American  Latin American problem other than when  we had the spotlight on Nicaraguan El  Salvador what is it about this country  that were well just by that much time on  a friend of mine said in terms of the  u.s. press there are no Latin stories  there's just us stories and Latin  settings and I think that's true for how  the United States tends to think about  Latin America we're interested in the  United States problems which are drugs  which is why we want to know what's  going on in Colombia when we were  fighting a war in Nicaragua we were  eager for news in El Salvador we're  interested because we put so much money  into that country's war but we don't  tend to think about the rest and I think  one reason is because we don't tend to  think that those are people like us but  they are one thing that I marveled at  for example I went to Medellin which is  the capital of the drug mafia and the  most violent city in the world not  involved in a war and the single leading  cause of death in Medellin is multiple  gunshot wounds and people have learned  to live with that level of violence  they've learned to think about it in the  passive voice as if it's a weather  condition that just sort of arrives and  doesn't have any perpetrator and I was  thinking how is it that people learn to  it to think about violence that way and  on my 10th day in Medellin  I met a man who was an ex-policeman and  we were talking and he said to me Tina  tomorrow I'm meeting with a friend of  mine a man named say sorry who wants to  kill his father his father's a bad man  and treats the family very badly and he  so he's hiring two hitmen to kill his  father and I'm setting up this deal do  you want to come along okay I said yeah  I'd love to so they all came to my hotel  and we all went up on the rooftop  restaurant and had a cup of coffee and  it was me in this cop this ex-cop and se  saw in these two teenage [ __ ] hitmen and  I interviewed them about what their  profession is and how they work and how  much they get paid and how they think  about it and how  their family thinks about it and then  they interviewed say sorry what time  does your father come home from work  what is his usual route this is he armed  does he have bodyguards does he have a  godfather in the neighborhood and then  we said our good-byes and the next day  it occurred to me that I had witnessed a  murder being planned and I hadn't  thought about it I hadn't made the  connection in my mind between the fact  that say sighs father was going to die  and these two young kids who were going  to kill him in ten days in Medellin I  learned to think like they do about  violence in Chile some of the people I  met who were the most sophisticated the  most civilized the most the closest to  my approximation of sophisticated people  in the United States you know fluent in  English and French and people who loved  opera  they were Pinochet's biggest supporters  and they were people who had who  justified and supported the violence of  the Pinochet government more than more  than anyone else it is people like us  who commit violence in Latin America and  Americans like me down there learn to  think like those people in a very short  period of time and I believe that if  more Americans realize that about Latin  America we would be more interested in  it because it is in many ways not a  separate race of people they're human  beings and they think like human beings  and they think like we do in a lot of  ways was it is it Judge Medina mm-hmm  who went after mr. Escobar and Colombia  Madine my on the right track here I just  want to ask you about one little  incident was it when the judge was  killed was it was it his nine-year-old  son that saw the that's regular hiding  behind the tree tell that story um tell  the whole story I mean I jumped it okay  well my main character in Colombia is  this judge who is an idealist is a man  who in 1984 was assigned the case that  would require him to indict Pablo  Escobar the head of the medellin  traffickers and he could have done with  what most judges did before him which is  to throw the case out  and therefore he could have gotten rich  and lived a very long life instead he  chose to indict Pablo Escobar knowing  that that was bringing a death sentence  on his head and for a solid year he got  death threats he got phone calls saying  judge Medina we know your children's  route home from school you know they  would recite to him passages of his  legal opinions um and he was finally  killed a year later and his son who was  nine witnessed the killing it was done  with with by in his front yard as his  wife was bringing him home from work one  day and now his his wife when I met her  is a lawyer who now wants to become a  judge and is interested in taking up a  similar line of work by the way what  does Quixote mean you know two refers to  Don Quixote which is the the famous  Spanish character of the man tilting at  windmills the idea lists the ultimate  idealist which was judgment in his  favorite book you have another book in  mind next um I would like to do another  book you know about I don't know yet are  you through writing about Latin America  I don't know um I am still very  fascinated by by Latin America I still  find it a country where the noises are  louder and the colors are brighter and  and and life is more vivid and intense  and the stories are better and all the  decisions in people's daily lives are  much more life or death than up here  it's still a fascinating place when you  come back up here to the United States  in Washington do you find many people  knowing much about that part of the  world in DC yes in general not so much  in general people's experience with it  is like is like mine was before I went  they skip the stories in the newspaper  the name sound the same you wonder why  they're so violent down there on the  back of your jacket here you have  congratulatory positive statements  written about your book from Greg  Easterbrook contributing editor of  Newsweek James Fallows author Atlantic  Monthly you also have mr. area mister  near re and I are from Human Rights  Watch yeah and also Robert pastor former  director of Latin Affairs National  Security Council how's that  the publishing house sends the  manuscript to various readers beforehand  and asks them for their comment does it  sell books they are under the impression  that it does why because people pick it  up and lecture you know that somebody  famous who you respect thinks this is a  good book so you're more inclined to buy  it especially for an author like me  where it's my first book and and if you  haven't heard of the author it helps to  have famous people plugging in on the  back does it ever work for you when you  go buying books probably it has sure  yeah El Salvador how much money a year  does the United States give in foreign  aid to El Salvador well I think at the  end of the 80s we were giving them  actually more money we were making up  more than half of their federal budget  we were putting in more money than they  were to their federal coffers I think  over the 10 years we gave them a total  of four billion dollars and that's for a  country where there are only 5 million  people so that's that's a tremendous  amount of money top 5 of the American  country countries that America gives  foreign aid to why it was an experiment  we were trying to to see if  counterinsurgency could work and at a  country that was tiny and small and  supposedly easy to maneuver in and we  thought we were going to get in and get  out and it was going to be simple and it  wasn't and after 10 years the war looks  not very different than it did in 1980  except for the fact that 70,000 people  have been killed and 1/2 not 1/5 of the  countries in exile and another 10th live  in refugee camps how much time you spend  there I think I probably made about six  or seven visits over the course of  several years where'd you go it's not a  big place it's the size of Massachusetts  so I think I probably covered most of  the country would you meet um well I I  was looking for rich people so I spend  my time going to aerobics classes I hung  out at Fitness World in San Salvador  which is very much like a fitness studio  in the United States with Garfield  posters on the wall and when one of the  members had a birthday we would sing  happy birthday in English to them and we  did our aerobics to Michael Jackson  records I went to pool parties I went to  discos that's not the experience I had  in most other countries but I was  deliberately trying to meet the rich and  I'll Salvador so I had quite a different  experience here than I did most other  places how did you do that um how did I  meet them yeah I mean you just show up  knock on their door and they know that  wasn't that wouldn't have worked the  original sorta don't love American  journalists they really felt that  American journalists were not writing  the truth and they used the word with  almost a capital T in El Salvador they  felt we were supporting communist forces  there and they were very reluctant to be  interviewed I met a group of people at a  rally for Christiani  who is the president of El Salvador now  and they call themselves the Qaddafi  gang the word comes from a Spanish  sentence quatre fin de semana no  sandwich camels every weekend we get  drunk and the first two words Caliph in  our sort of like Qaddafi so they call  themselves the Qaddafi gang and it was  eight women all of them in their 30s I  think all of them divorced all of them  blonde and very very wealthy and various  of their male friends and they met every  weekend and I met these people at a  rally and they invited me to some of  their parties so I got to hang out with  them and start to understand how they  live and how they think they know what  you were doing they knew that I was  writing about their country and that I  was eager to talk to the wealthy um yeah  I think it what I was doing  did you have any ground rules um yes the  main character that I ended up writing  about did not want his name used so I  have changed his name as I indicated in  the book his name in the book is his  name in the book is Roberto Cotter and  why did he want his name changed for the  out um he wanted his his name changed  because he felt he wouldn't like what I  was writing which I that's not his name  in the book wasn't what's his name in  the book oh I haven't underlying here  somewhere I'll try to find it going back  to the Elser about trying I think it's  l-tron yes  Kara that's the name of a Peruvian I  think I interviewed so when you got  inside the group what was the first  thing you started to see oh how they  live these palatial  mansions with filled with pre-columbian  art and multiple swimming pools I will  never forget a pool party 